{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 14,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -1,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4
    ,
      "why": "iterative is the area-cheapest family (shared datapath, no k-way replication). It is the only family that can plausibly minimise luts_plus_ffs while meeting max_abs_err <= 2^-12. data_width 14-20 and n_iter 12-20 bracket the accuracy requirement (error ~ 2^-(n_iter+angle_guard) and output quantisation 2^-(W-2)); W=14 gives 2^-12 LSB which is the tightest area point that can still hit the error bound with enough iterations. angle_guard -1..2 and frac_guard 0..2 cover the accuracy knobs without wasting budget on guards that only add area. Rounding is a cheap binary knob that shifts error by ~1 LSB. Throughput is NOT a binding constraint for iterative here: the 32-request burst is issued at once and must return within 0.44 us, which is a latency/batch constraint, not a sustained-throughput one, so iterative's N+3 cycles/result is acceptable as long as the batch fits the window."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 14,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -1,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 6
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k trades area for latency: k chained micro-rotations/cycle cuts the N+3 schedule to ceil(N/k)+3, which is what the 0.44 us p99-batch window and the throughput_msps >= 32 constraint actually pressure. Since the 32 requests arrive simultaneously, the unit must sustain ~32 results/us, i.e. one result per cycle at >= 32 MHz; unrolled_k with k>=2 and a high enough Fmax is the realistic way to hit that while staying in my assigned families. k=2..6 covers the latency/area trade-off (k=2 is near-iterative area, k=6 is the latency extreme). Same accuracy ranges as iterative because accuracy is family-independent; the search here is really over k and the accuracy knobs jointly. Giving it the larger share reflects that the binding constraints (throughput, batch latency) are latency-driven, so the interesting Pareto points for this spec live in unrolled_k."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so this is a cold start. The spec has two hard, latency/throughput-driven constraints (throughput_msps >= 32 and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 for a 32-wide simultaneous burst) plus an accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-12), and the objective is to minimise luts_plus_ffs. Within my assigned families (iterative, unrolled_k), iterative is the area floor but its N+3 cycles/result schedule is the risk for the throughput/batch window; unrolled_k is the family that can meet the latency/throughput constraints by parallelising micro-rotations. I therefore search both, with the larger share on unrolled_k because the binding constraints are latency-driven. Accuracy ranges are kept tight (data_width 14-20, n_iter 12-20, angle_guard -1..2, frac_guard 0..2) because accuracy is family-independent and the 2^-12 floor pins W and N to a narrow band; wasting budget on W=28 or N=30 would only inflate area with no accuracy benefit. Rounding is included as a cheap binary knob since it shifts error by ~1 LSB and can let a slightly smaller W/N pass the error bound. I deliberately do not push data_width or n_iter to their registry maxima, since the objective is area minimisation and the error bound is the only thing that sets the accuracy floor. The 0.44 us batch window at the control tick means the unit must clear 32 results quickly; unrolled_k's reduced schedule is the lever, so it gets 60% of the budget to map the latency-vs-area trade-off, while iterative gets 40% to anchor the area-cheapest corner of the front."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The iterative and unrolled_k families are fundamentally incapable of meeting the throughput constraint. The spec requires throughput_msps >= 32, but the best observed throughput for iterative is 12.1 MSPS and for unrolled_k is 9.76 MSPS. Since iterative processes 1 result per N+3 cycles and unrolled_k processes 1 result per ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, their throughput is limited by the number of cycles per result. Even with high Fmax, the cycle latency prevents them from reaching 32 MSPS (which requires ~1 result per 31.25ns or less, effectively 1-2 cycles per result at typical Fmax). The pipelined and pipelined_m families produce 1 result per cycle, which is the only way to achieve the required 32 MSPS throughput. Additionally, the sys_p99_batch_us constraint (<= 0.44 us) for 32 requests implies a very high sustained throughput and low latency, which only pipelined architectures can satisfy. Therefore, I must add the pipelined family to the exploration.",
